[
  {
    "question": "Can I use the Hermes web dashboard on native Windows, and are there any platform-specific limitations to keep in mind?",
    "answer": "Yes, Hermes includes a first-party web dashboard that can be launched with `hermes dashboard` to open a local server at `http://127.0.0.1:9119`. While native Windows 10/11 is a Tier 1 supported platform using `install.ps1`, the dashboard's embedded `/chat` terminal requires a POSIX PTY, so WSL2 is still preferred if you need that specific pane.",
    "evidence": [
      "`website/docs/user-guide/features/web-dashboard.md:21` says it starts a local web server and opens `http://127.0.0.1:9119` in the browser.",
      "the dashboard `/chat` embedded terminal requires a POSIX PTY, so WSL2 is still preferred for that pane."
    ]
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I configure a local Ollama vision model so that Hermes connects to the local instance without API key conflicts and enables image message support?",
    "answer": "To prevent Hermes from scanning environment variables and routing calls to external providers, explicitly set `provider: custom` along with your local `base_url` (e.g., `http://localhost:11434`). Additionally, because custom-provider endpoints do not automatically declare vision support, configure `supports_vision: true` in your model configuration to enable native image payloads.",
    "evidence": [
      "Setting `provider: custom` forces Hermes to use your `base_url` instead of auto-detecting from environment variables.",
      "setting `supports_vision: true` in your model config should enable native image payloads for Ollama vision models."
    ]
  },
  {
    "question": "How does subagent delegation work in Hermes, and where are subagent models configured given Hermes' model-routing policies?",
    "answer": "Multi-agent communication is performed via the `delegate_task` tool, which spawns isolated child AIAgent instances with their own context, toolsets, and terminal sessions (supporting up to 3 concurrent subagents by default). Because Hermes rejects automatic complexity-based model routing and per-turn provider overrides, subagent models must be explicitly configured under `delegation.provider` and `delegation.model` in `config.yaml`.",
    "evidence": [
      "Spawns isolated child AIAgent instances with their own context, toolsets, and terminal sessions",
      "The supported configuration point for subagents remains `delegation.provider` / `delegation.model` in `config.yaml`."
    ]
  },
  {
    "question": "How can I deploy Hermes using Docker on an ARM64 NAS device without running into image architecture or volume bind-mount permission issues?",
    "answer": "Hermes publishes multi-arch Docker images covering both `linux/amd64` and `linux/arm64` natively. For NAS environments (such as UGOS Pro, Synology, or unRAID) where bind mounts can cause permission denied errors, Hermes supports the `PUID` and `PGID` environment variables (aliasing `HERMES_UID`/`HERMES_GID`) to remap container user permissions before runtime services start.",
    "evidence": [
      "publishes a multi-arch manifest covering `linux/amd64` and `linux/arm64`.",
      "`48083211ef606f3305c09df576514ac99bc7f594` added `PUID`/`PGID` aliases for `HERMES_UID`/`HERMES_GID`, preserving canonical-variable precedence."
    ]
  },
  {
    "question": "How can I enable observability tracing in Hermes, and why is reducing active toolsets helpful when running smaller local models?",
    "answer": "You can enable the bundled Langfuse observability plugin via `hermes tools` or `hermes plugins enable observability/langfuse` to record turn traces, tool inputs, and outputs. For smaller local models (e.g., 4B–9B), using `hermes tools` to disable unused toolsets improves latency because the agent sends the entire tool schema (~45 tools) on every API call, consuming substantial input tokens.",
    "evidence": [
      "the agent sends the full tool schema (~45 tools) on every API call, which is a lot of input tokens for a 4B-9B model.",
      "`plugins/observability/langfuse/README.md:3-17` documents enabling it with `hermes tools` or `hermes plugins enable observability/langfuse`."
    ]
  },
  {
    "question": "How do you configure the Discord gateway interactively, and how does the agent process code attachments like Python files uploaded to Discord?",
    "answer": "You can set up Discord interactively by running `hermes gateway setup`, which presents a platform picker, prompts for `DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN`, and sets `DISCORD_ALLOWED_USERS`. Once active, authorized attachments of small text-readable files—including Python scripts (`.py`) and `.txt` files—are automatically injected directly into the agent's prompt.",
    "evidence": [
      "The setup flow presents a messaging-platform picker and dispatches the selected platform through `_configure_platform()`",
      "`gateway/platforms/base.py:1370`–`1383` explicitly includes `.py` in the text-injection extensions, covering uploaded Python scripts as well as `.txt` files."
    ]
  },
  {
    "question": "How does Hermes send media generated by MCP tools and deliver generated document files across Matrix and Email gateways?",
    "answer": "When MCP tools return images, Hermes caches the base64 content and surfaces `MEDIA:<path>` tags in the tool results for native platform delivery. When delivering generated local documents across messaging gateways, Hermes dispatches them via adapter methods like `send_document`, sending files as `m.file` media on Matrix and as SMTP multipart attachments on Email.",
    "evidence": [
      "`tools/mcp_tool.py:673` caches base64 `ImageContent` blocks and emits `MEDIA:<path>`; `tools/mcp_tool.py:3958`–`3977` includes those tags in MCP tool results.",
      "`plugins/platforms/matrix/adapter.py:1950` uploads documents as Matrix `m.file` media; `tools/send_message_tool.py:1734-1748` dispatches Matrix media files to that method."
    ]
  },
  {
    "question": "How can I run long autonomous tasks beyond standard turn caps, and what happens to Telegram conversation history during a crash or gateway restart?",
    "answer": "Interactive conversations are bounded by `agent.max_turns` (default 90, overridable with `hermes chat --max-turns <N>`), but autonomous cross-turn tasks can be executed using `/goal` (which persists and can be continued with `/goal resume`). In Telegram, restarts or interruptions mark sessions as `resume_pending` to preserve the session ID and transcript rather than wiping the conversation.",
    "evidence": [
      "`agent.max_turns` defaults to 90 and can be changed in config; `hermes chat --max-turns <N>` overrides it for one conversation turn",
      "`f1e029251` changed crash/restart handling from blanket suspension to `resume_pending`, preserving the existing conversation instead of wiping it"
    ]
  },
  {
    "question": "What is the policy for contributing custom memory providers or tools, and where should personal plugins be placed and enabled?",
    "answer": "Hermes is closed to new in-tree additions in `plugins/memory/` and encourages personal tools to be built as plugins rather than core edits. Custom memory providers (implementing `MemoryProvider` ABC) and custom tools should be placed as standalone plugins in `~/.hermes/plugins/` (or installed via pip entry points) and explicitly enabled using `hermes plugins enable <name>`.",
    "evidence": [
      "PRs adding to `plugins/memory/` will be closed. Publish as standalone plugin into `~/.hermes/plugins/` or via pip entry point.",
      "user plugins are scanned from `~/.hermes/plugins` in `hermes_cli/plugins.py:1192`, and standalone/user plugins are opt-in via `plugins.enabled`"
    ]
  },
  {
    "question": "Where does Hermes store user identity preferences versus agent persona, and how can you inspect which skills have been executed?",
    "answer": "The agent's persona is defined in `SOUL.md`, whereas user identity and preferences are stored in `USER.md` via memory operations using `target=\"user\"`. Skill usage metrics (such as view counts, use counts, and timestamps) are saved in `~/.hermes/skills/.usage.json` and can be inspected in the Top Skills report using `/insights` or `hermes insights`.",
    "evidence": [
      "`tools/memory_tool.py:5` and `:280` define `USER.md` as the persistent user-profile store and map `target=\"user\"` to that file",
      "`tools/skill_usage.py:1` documents the skill usage sidecar at `~/.hermes/skills/.usage.json`, including `use_count`, `view_count`, and timestamps."
    ]
  },
  {
    "question": "What dependency group does Hermes install on Termux, and why does `hermes update` sometimes appear to hang during Node.js dependency updates?",
    "answer": "When installing or updating Hermes on Termux, the installer automatically selects the curated `termux-all` (or `termux`) dependency group instead of desktop packages. During `hermes update`, the Node.js update step can appear stuck because the Camofox postinstall step silently downloads a browser binary without displaying real-time progress.",
    "evidence": [
      "`hermes_cli/main.py:9907-9947` makes `hermes update` select the curated `termux-all` dependency group on Termux.",
      "npm install appears stuck during Camofox postinstall (downloads browser binary silently with no progress output)."
    ]
  },
  {
    "question": "How do you allow specific Telegram users to DM the bot, and can you set distinct personas for different chat channels?",
    "answer": "To authorize a direct message user on Telegram, specify their user ID under `allow_from` in `config.yaml` (which sets `TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_USERS`), as `allowed_chats` only serves as a group-chat response whitelist. To configure different personas across specific channels or groups, define `channel_prompts` in your platform configuration to provide distinct ephemeral system prompts per channel ID.",
    "evidence": [
      "`allowed_chats` is **not** the user-authorization key — that's why it had no effect. It's a *group-chat response whitelist*",
      "Set distinct ephemeral system prompts per channel/group ID in your platform config"
    ]
  },
  {
    "question": "What is the supported authentication setup for Google Gemini, and how does Hermes retrieve streaming token usage for OpenAI-compatible providers?",
    "answer": "The deprecated Gemini CLI OAuth flow has been removed; Gemini is now configured using Google AI Studio with `GOOGLE_API_KEY` or `GEMINI_API_KEY`. For streaming OpenAI-compatible chat endpoints, Hermes tracks token usage by automatically transmitting `stream_options={\"include_usage\": true}` on chat completion requests (excluding native Gemini endpoints that reject the parameter).",
    "evidence": [
      "Current supported Gemini setup is Google AI Studio with `GOOGLE_API_KEY` or `GEMINI_API_KEY`",
      "`agent/chat_completion_helpers.py:2222-2229` now sends `stream_options={\"include_usage\": true}` for streaming OpenAI-compatible chat-completions requests"
    ]
  },
  {
    "question": "What endpoint does Hermes use for Ollama Cloud, and how are local-only memory backends like Honcho handled without remote API keys?",
    "answer": "Hermes connects to Ollama Cloud via its built-in `ollama-cloud` provider using the OpenAI-compatible endpoint `https://ollama.com/v1`. For local plugins such as Honcho, Hermes allows local operation without external API keys by accepting `base_url` pointing to localhost and automatically injecting `\"local\"` as a placeholder API key at client initialization.",
    "evidence": [
      "`43802ef3e37826af8fb67b98208051aea916a48a` added the Ollama Cloud default endpoint `https://ollama.com/v1`.",
      "whenever `base_url` points to `localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, or `::1` — preventing the \"Invalid API key\" SDK validation error entirely"
    ]
  },
  {
    "question": "How does Hermes manage multi-user profile isolation in gateways, and how does the DingTalk connector deliver image URLs?",
    "answer": "Hermes supports multi-profile gateway multiplexing, running adapters for secondary profiles under each profile's own home directory and credentials to isolate sessions, memory, and skills. When sending images over DingTalk, the adapter renders remote image URLs into DingTalk Markdown payloads using `![image](...)` syntax.",
    "evidence": [
      "`gateway/run.py:8455` starts enabled adapters for secondary profiles under each profile's own home and credential scope",
      "`gateway/platforms/dingtalk.py:948` renders the URL as `![image](...)`, and `gateway/platforms/dingtalk.py:900` sends replies as DingTalk `msgtype: markdown` payloads."
    ]
  }
]
